# Local overrides for `make run-orbital`. Copy to deploy/local/orbital.env
# (gitignored) and fill in your OWN identity provider's values — every value
# below is a placeholder and points at nothing.
#
# The file is optional. Absent, orbital still runs and local password login
# still works, so a fresh clone needs no setup at all.

# ── Browser SSO: orbital as an OAuth client ───────────────────────────────────
# Drives orbital's own login flow. Leave unset to disable SSO; password login is
# unaffected. The redirect URL must match what you registered with the provider
# byte-for-byte — OAuth compares it exactly.
ORBITAL_OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://idp.example.com/realms/<realm>
ORBITAL_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<orbital-client-id>
ORBITAL_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>
ORBITAL_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:8001/auth/callback

# ── Bearer auth: orbital as a resource server ─────────────────────────────────
# Verifying bearer tokens locally needs BOTH lines below.
#
# ORBITAL_API_AUTH_ENABLED now defaults to TRUE in the binary — `make run-orbital`
# opts a fresh clone DOWN to false so it runs with no setup. Setting it true here
# is what makes the provider list actually be consulted; without it tokens are
# accepted unchecked and a provider list looks broken when it is simply unused.
#
# ORBITAL_TEMPLATE_HOT_RELOAD_ENABLED replaced ORBITAL_DEV (2026-09-23). The old
# flag also decided API auth and whether the placeholder session key was accepted;
# each is now its own flag with its own safe default. The Makefile already turns
# hot-reload on for `make run-orbital`, so this line is only needed if you run
# ./cmd/orbital directly.
ORBITAL_TEMPLATE_HOT_RELOAD_ENABLED=true
ORBITAL_API_AUTH_ENABLED=true

# The providers orbital trusts, as a single-line JSON array. This is the ONLY
# source of bearer verification — unset, there is none, and with API auth enabled
# orbital refuses to start. ORBITAL_OIDC_* above drives the browser login only.
# Two shapes are shown: an upstream service that authorizes its own users
# (delegatedAuthorization — every valid token gets one role, no user row), and
# human callers whose role
# comes from a group claim with `defaultRole` as the floor for an unmatched
# token — drop `defaultRole` there to refuse those instead.
# Full reference: docs/reference/AUTH.md § Multiple identity providers.
#
# NOTE the single quotes. `make run-orbital` SOURCES this file (`set -a; . file`),
# so an unquoted value has its inner double quotes stripped by the shell and
# orbital refuses to start with "ORBITAL_AUTH_PROVIDERS is not valid JSON".
#ORBITAL_AUTH_PROVIDERS='[{"issuer":{"url":"https://idp.example.com/realms/<realm>","audiences":["<orbital-client-id>"]},"clientID":"<upstream-service-client-id>","claimMappings":{"username":{"claim":"email"}},"delegatedAuthorization":{"role":"admin"}},{"issuer":{"url":"https://idp.example.com/realms/<realm>","audiences":["<orbital-client-id>"]},"clientID":"<orbital-client-id>","claimMappings":{"username":{"claim":"email"},"groups":{"claim":"<groups-claim>"}},"roleMapping":[{"group":"<admin-group>","role":"admin"}],"defaultRole":"readonly"}]'
